---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

const services = [
  { name: 'Vaultwarden', desc: 'Password vault', port: ':8081' },
  { name: 'Gitea', desc: 'Self-hosted git + CI', port: ':8087' },
  { name: 'Headscale', desc: 'Self-hosted mesh control plane', port: ':8086' },
  { name: 'Pi-hole', desc: 'DNS ad-blocking', port: ':8053' },
  { name: 'Samba + Syncthing', desc: 'Centralized storage + sync', port: ':445 / :22000' },
  { name: 'Uptime Kuma', desc: 'Service monitoring', port: ':8082' },
  { name: 'Netdata', desc: 'Real-time system metrics', port: ':19999' },
];
---

<Base title="Homelab Case Study">
  <h1 class="font-display text-4xl font-bold mb-4 text-brand-dark">The Homelab Rebuild</h1>
  <p class="text-lg text-text-muted mb-6 max-w-2xl">
    It started with a locked door. A password vault I couldn't reach, because a third-party
    coordination server had an outage and took my access with it. I rebuilt the whole thing
    self-hosted &mdash; then, once it worked, deliberately tore half of it back down.
  </p>

  <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-6 mb-16 reveal">
    <div class="border border-surface-light rounded-2xl px-6 py-4 bg-surface-light/20">
      <div class="font-display text-3xl font-bold text-accent">30 &rarr; 9</div>
      <div class="text-xs text-text-muted mt-1">Containers, peak vs. live today</div>
    </div>
    <div class="border border-surface-light rounded-2xl px-6 py-4 bg-surface-light/20">
      <div class="font-display text-3xl font-bold text-accent">2 &rarr; 1</div>
      <div class="text-xs text-text-muted mt-1">Nodes, two-machine mesh vs. one Pi</div>
    </div>
    <div class="border border-surface-light rounded-2xl px-6 py-4 bg-surface-light/20">
      <div class="font-display text-3xl font-bold text-accent">1</div>
      <div class="text-xs text-text-muted mt-1">Leaked API key found and revoked mid-rebuild</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <section class="mb-16 reveal">
    <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold mb-6 text-brand">The arc</h2>
    <div class="space-y-6">
      <div class="border-l-2 border-surface-light pl-6">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-brand-light">The outage</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-text-muted mt-1">
          A third-party mesh-networking service (Tailscale's coordination server) went down,
          and with it went access to a self-hosted password vault. That's the actual reason this
          exists &mdash; not a weekend project, a real lockout.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="border-l-2 border-surface-light pl-6">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-brand-light">v1.0 — Single Pi, Compose only</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-text-muted mt-1">Rebuilt deliberately minimal: one Pi 4B, 4GB RAM, Docker Compose. No orchestrator.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="border-l-2 border-surface-light pl-6">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-brand-light">Multi-node ambition, tested and rejected</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-text-muted mt-1">
          K3s, ArgoCD, and Helmfile were evaluated for orchestration, then removed.
          K3s alone costs ~600&ndash;800MB idle RAM &mdash; too much overhead on a 4GB box
          before running a single real workload.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="border-l-2 border-surface-light pl-6">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-brand-light">The big offload</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-text-muted mt-1">
          A second node (Windows, 16GB RAM) took on the full observability + secrets stack:
          Grafana, Prometheus, Loki, Tempo, Alertmanager, Authelia, Infisical &mdash; 11 containers.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="border-l-2 border-accent pl-6">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-accent">The security find</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-text-muted mt-1">
          Auditing the rebuild surfaced a live Headscale API key hardcoded in client-side JavaScript
          on this very site, readable by anyone on the network. Revoked it at the source same day,
          rewrote the page to stop shipping secrets to the browser.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="border-l-2 border-accent pl-6">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-accent">The retirement</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-text-muted mt-1">
          The 11-container stack ran, then got switched off. Single-operator homelab, no multi-user
          access-control need, no rotation workflow ever actually used. Uptime Kuma covers what
          actually gets checked day to day. Written up as a formal decision record before deleting
          anything, not after.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="border-l-2 border-surface-light pl-6">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-brand-light">Where it landed</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-text-muted mt-1">
          A lean core of 9 services that get used daily, a single-container Netdata swapped in for the
          six-container metrics stack it replaced, and a retirement pattern where nothing gets silently
          deleted &mdash; decommissioned code moves through a holding folder before it's gone for good.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="mb-16 reveal">
    <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold mb-6 text-brand">Live services</h2>
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3 gap-4">
      {services.map((svc) => (
        <div class="card-lift border border-surface-light rounded-2xl p-4 bg-surface-light/20">
          <div class="flex items-center justify-between mb-1">
            <h3 class="font-semibold text-sm">{svc.name}</h3>
            <span class="text-xs font-mono text-accent">{svc.port}</span>
          </div>
          <p class="text-xs text-text-muted">{svc.desc}</p>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
    <p class="text-xs text-text-muted mt-4">
      Every service above is reachable at its own address directly over an encrypted mesh network &mdash;
      no reverse proxy, no single point of failure for access to everything at once.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="reveal">
    <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold mb-6 text-brand">Key design decisions</h2>
    <ul class="space-y-3 text-text-muted mb-8">
      <li class="flex gap-3"><span class="text-accent font-bold shrink-0">No K3s</span><span>Control plane alone costs 600&ndash;800MB idle RAM on a 4GB Pi</span></li>
      <li class="flex gap-3"><span class="text-accent font-bold shrink-0">No reverse proxy</span><span>Direct mesh-IP:port per service — one less thing that can break access to everything at once</span></li>
      <li class="flex gap-3"><span class="text-accent font-bold shrink-0">No auth layer</span><span>Mesh membership is the access boundary — no multi-user need to justify SSO infrastructure</span></li>
      <li class="flex gap-3"><span class="text-accent font-bold shrink-0">Retire, don't delete</span><span>Everything decommissioned moves through a holding folder before removal — recoverable via git history</span></li>
    </ul>

    <div class="border border-surface-light rounded-2xl p-6 bg-brand/5">
      <p class="text-text-muted mb-4">
        What this actually demonstrates: operating under a hard resource constraint, evaluating
        real trade-offs instead of defaulting to "more tools," catching a security issue in my own
        work and fixing it same-day, and reversing a decision in public instead of quietly walking it back.
      </p>
      <a href="https://github.com/iamvanshkhanna/homelab-ops-mesh" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 text-brand-dark font-semibold hover:underline">
        See the real commit history &rarr;
      </a>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
